<Doc>
    <Section title="`route.query` keeps a page's state in the address">
        <Example
            id="navigate"
            app="crm"
            title="`route.query(defaults)` — the query as one value, written by navigating"
            summary="A company page keeps its open tab in the address, so a link can open it. Type a draft, then switch tabs: the address moves, and the draft stays."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.abide': companyTabsSource,
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTabs.test.ts': companyTabsTestSource,
                'e2e/CompanyTabs.test.ts': companyTabsSpecSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompanyTabs />
        </Example>

        <p><code>route.query</code> reads each member its defaults name off the address, as the
            type of its default: a number, <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>, text, or a list of
            text from a name repeated. A member missing, or one that does not parse, reads as its
            default. A member the defaults do not name is left in the address for whoever reads it.</p>

        <p>Assign one member, <code>query.tab = name</code>, and only that member changes. Assign the
            whole value, <code>query = &#123; tab: 'notes' &#125;</code>, and every member you leave
            out goes back to its default. Either write is one navigation that writes over the history
            entry, so Back leaves the company rather than stepping through every tab, and a member at
            its default is left out of the address. On a server it reads the request's address, and a
            write throws, as <code>navigate</code> refuses there.</p>

        <p>An address that matches the page already shown moves <code>route.url</code> and keeps
            the page mounted. Another page's address mounts that page in place, without reloading the
            document. A plain <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> click does the same, and so does
            <code>navigate(address, &#123; replace, keepScroll &#125;)</code>. Reach for
            <code>navigate</code> when code decides where to go. <code>route.navigating</code> is
            true while a navigation is in flight, which is what a progress bar reads.</p>

        <p><code>navigate(-1)</code> steps back through the history the way <code>history.go</code>
            does, and its promise settles once the page it steps to is on screen.
            <code>route.index</code> is the entry's place in the history the app wrote: a step back of
            up to that many entries stays inside the app.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
